#ifndef INDIRECT_BOUNCE_GLSLH
#define INDIRECT_BOUNCE_GLSLH

// THE one-bounce estimate: what ONE already-shaded surface sends to another. The algebra half of the
// screen-space GI gather in Programs/Deferred/DeferredLighting.shader, split out of it for exactly the
// reason Mesh/AmbientIBL.glslh is split — it declares no sampler and no uniform block, so it compiles
// as C++ and Desert/Tests/Engine/IndirectBounce asserts its energy bound on the SHIPPED TEXT instead of
// on a paraphrase of it.
//
// The defect that made it a file. The gather shaded the bouncing neighbour with
// `albedo * max(0.0, dot(N, sunL)) * radiance` — the un-normalized Lambert form the forward mesh
// shaders shipped until Mesh/DirectLighting.glslh replaced it — so the emitter of every bounce was PI
// times brighter than the same surface is when the main pass shades it, and a white one radiated 3.14
// times the light that reached it. It survived the sweep that removed that form everywhere else
// because the census asked whether each shader FILE names the shared BRDF: DeferredLighting.shader
// does, for its sun, one function below this one. The middle link kept its own copy and no assertion
// was about the middle link.
//
// A bounce is direct light seen a second time. It takes the same BRDF, and now it takes the same text.
//
// Requires Mesh/DirectLighting.glslh (and through it Mesh/PBRFunctions.glslh) to have been included
// first.

// The light the surface at `receiverPos` receives from the surface at `emitterPos`, both already shaded
// by the sun. Returned as INCIDENT light, which is what Mesh/AmbientIBL.glslh's ComposeAmbient expects
// for its `indirect` argument — the receiver's own albedo is applied there, not here.
//
//   receiverPos / receiverN — the surface being shaded; N unit, world space.
//   emitterPos / emitterN   — the neighbouring surface doing the bouncing; N unit, world space.
//   emitterAlbedo / emitterF0 / emitterMetalness / emitterRoughness
//                           — its material, read from the same G-buffer the main pass shades it from.
//                             F0 is mix(Fdielectric, albedo, metalness), as at every other call site;
//                             roughness must already be clamped away from 0 by the caller.
//   sunL                    — UNIT vector toward the sun, the same one the main pass shades with.
//   sunRadiance             — the sun's incident radiance at the emitter.
vec3 EvaluateBounceSample(vec3 receiverPos, vec3 receiverN, vec3 emitterPos, vec3 emitterN,
                          vec3 emitterAlbedo, vec3 emitterF0, float emitterMetalness,
                          float emitterRoughness, vec3 sunL, vec3 sunRadiance)
{
	vec3  toEmitter = emitterPos - receiverPos;
	float d2        = dot(toEmitter, toEmitter);
	if (d2 <= 0.0)
		return vec3(0.0);

	vec3 dn = normalize(toEmitter);

	// The two halves of the form factor: neither surface can send or receive through its own back.
	float recv = max(dot(receiverN, dn), 0.0);
	float emit = max(dot(emitterN, -dn), 0.0);
	if (recv <= 0.0 || emit <= 0.0)
		return vec3(0.0);

	// -dn is the emitter's VIEW vector — the direction from it toward the surface being shaded. The
	// estimate is therefore NOT viewless, and the whole shared BRDF applies rather than a diffuse half
	// of it prised out by hand: a polished floor throwing the sun onto a wall is part of the bounce, and
	// a metal one correctly contributes no diffuse at all instead of bouncing its own reflectance as
	// paint.
	vec3 outgoing = EvaluateDirectLight(sunL, sunRadiance, -dn, emitterN, emitterF0, emitterMetalness,
	                                    emitterRoughness, emitterAlbedo);

	// Geometric coupling: the two cosines above, and a softened inverse square.
	//
	// THE `1.0` IS IN SQUARE WORLD UNITS AND THE WORLD IS MEASURED IN CENTIMETRES, so the bounce is
	// half-strength one centimetre away and is down by 1e-4 at a metre. That is why nothing in the
	// repository has ever seen this term: measured on CornellDemo (the scene that exists for colour
	// bleed) at the shipped GIIntensity of 2.0, the whole screen-space GI moves the final frame by at
	// most 1/255 on 0.21% of pixels, and the isolated indirect buffer at a hundred times the intensity
	// still peaks at 26/255. Rescaling this softening to metres (`1.0 + d2 * 1e-4`) moves 23.7% of the
	// frame by up to 48/255 and the green wall's bleed appears on the floor for the first time.
	//
	// It is left alone here deliberately. Changing it re-authors every scene in the repository — 49 of
	// 51 run this gather at the C++ default and not one has ever set the knob — so it is the owner's
	// call and not a correction to smuggle in beside a BRDF fix. See the Д17 report.
	float falloff = 1.0 + d2;

	return outgoing * recv * emit / falloff;
}

#endif // INDIRECT_BOUNCE_GLSLH
